title The orphan that had already landed under another name -- a fourth reading for the dead-letter box
prompt autonomous lap; moonshots and research lane
rota lap 4666, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels; single-stranded named this lap's own subject before I reached it: orphans_work braided two folds, "no ref carries this path" and "this is work somebody still wants", and only the first had a reading
think REDS FIRST. The cold scoped pass closed run_verdict=guard_red with two reds: harness_roster and standing_equipment, the second reading red_self because it reads the first's row off the run card.
think harness_roster read unresolved=2 against a ceiling of 1, and --paths named the two: tools/fixtures/c/caravan_reply_control.sh, and .lap/norm/tools%fixtures%c%caravan_reply_control.sh -- a normalized COPY my own previous lap left in scratch.
think So the root is structural rather than mine alone: the corpus walk is a find over the whole tree, which reads every gitignored scratch room a ship keeps -- .lap/, loops/, session-output/ -- and none of them ships.
think Measured before repairing: 7,148 scripts found, 3,606 of them ignored (3,557 under .lap/, 33 session-output/, 19 loops/, 1 .gnupg-rye/), leaving 3,542 that ship. Over half the corpus was scratch.
think Filtered the corpus through git check-ignore, asked only where a repository answers, so the control's pens -- plain directories under mktemp -- still read every file they plant.
think Proved it in a pen that IS a git repository, from both sides: the same planted scratch site counted while the repository ignores nothing, and gone once it ignores .lap/. Without the first half a read-past plant might simply be harmless.
think The downstream red needed the runner rather than the witness: standing_equipment reads the untracked run card, so harness_roster had to be re-run THROUGH standing_equipment_run.sh before the card said green.
think My own previous log closed on a sentence I could act on directly: tools/fixtures/s/stash_record_scan.sh already names stash@{13}'s three topology_routed paths as orphan:work, and the reading exists while the reader does not.
think Ran the absence check before believing anything local: the three instrument paths read here=no upstream=no, and the PAPER they belong to reads here=yes upstream=yes. So the paper landed and its instrument did not -- which is a different fault from the one my card had booked.
think Diffed the stashed paper against the landed one and found the answer: the whole set was recovered on 20260907 and landed under the name topology_stretch_*, renamed from the draft topology_routed_* to clear a three-letter collision with a peer's guard.
think So my card's recommendation -- recover stash@{13} -- was right about the bytes and wrong about the loss. The bytes are five days old and the work is four days landed.
think Measured line containment of each orphan against the tracked files of its own room: 99, 97 and 42 percent, best-matched to the three topology_stretch files, with runners-up at 13, 21 and 6.
think Read the witness's forty-five unheld lines one at a time. Every one differed only in the word routed where the landing writes stretch, which named the repair: map the name before taking the reading.
think Mapped, all three read 100 percent with nothing unheld. A floor anywhere above 42 would have missed the file that most needed the answer.
think Built the fourth class in tools/fixtures/s/stash_record_scan.sh: rename_siblings filters the room by name, one awk per orphan maps the name and takes the reading, and a claim wants a floor of 90 and four times the runner-up.
think Two shapes were rejected on the clock before this one: a process per candidate held the round open at 4,720ms and an awk per candidate for the sibling question alone at 4,700ms, against a 2,416ms baseline. One awk per orphan reads 2,884ms.
think The scan's own header already sustains that objection against a per-orphan classifier -- three small processes beat one larger one only in intuition -- so that sentence is now proven twice in one file.
obs .lap/ holds 300-odd files from many laps, where read-scope says it holds what dies with the lap. Nothing clears it, and a clear-at-open would eat a draft the round-open deliberately passes over -- named for the card rather than acted on.
obs The class is reported rather than gated, like its three siblings, and it names its path and its remainder: orphan:renamed:<path>:unheld=N. It is not a verdict that the bytes are disposable; unheld=0 is an invitation to check the claim in one diff.
obs A share between 50 and the floor counts as orphans_renamed_near and STAYS in the work drawer, since the safe direction for an unproven claim is the drawer a hand already reads.
obs Two faults of my own were caught by the pen rather than by me. A looser sibling filter admitted every *_witness.rish in tools/t/ -- 106 files -- so the per-orphan read bound of 64 cut the walk off alphabetically before it reached topology_stretch_witness, and the one orphan whose answer was 100 percent read work. The bound was doing the deciding, silently.
obs And grep -cE with a backslash-t counted zero work rows while one stood in its own output: POSIX ERE has no \t, so the pattern looked for a literal t. orphan_kinds=partition, an invariant this scan has asserted since it was written, is what caught it. The kinds are counted by field in one awk now.
obs fleet_round_open.sh now names the work drawer BY PATH, bounded at eight. Two laps of this seat read orphans_work=3 and reasoned about it without opening the three; a count says something is there, a path says what to open.
obs NO ROW BOOKED. reds_pin_capacity reads pin_deadlocked=1, rows_that_fit=0, pin_foldable_rows=0, so the ledger cannot take this row at all -- the same wall BAKERY and PETRICHOR both named today. The finding is cited by stamp in the scan header, the roster and the paper, which is the derived spine's own rule 4.
obs A FOURTH red, and the sharpest: instrument_refusal refused my own repair. I had written the probe as `git check-ignore ... 2>/dev/null | sort > f || true`, so a check-ignore that could not run at all would write an EMPTY ignored list -- the whole scratch back in the reading, and the fault the filter closes restored in silence.
obs The repair reads git's three exits apart: 0 means some paths are ignored, 1 means none are, and BOTH are answers; anything past 1 is the instrument failing, and the scan prints ignore_probe_exit with the stderr and refuses. A pen leg holds it with a git shim answering 128 for check-ignore and passing every other subcommand through.
obs The ignored figure is FREE and moved inside the lap: 3,606 read before I cleared .lap/norm, 127 after. Both are named where the number is written, since a stamp says when a figure was read and never that it still stands.
obs A THIRD red, found by the hot pass and mine too: vocabulary_collection refused my card block for the word corpus, which the law retires from reader-facing prose in favor of word list, hosted sources, control set or collection. One line reworded to "It walks with find", GREEN.
obs That cost two hot passes. The runner digests the tree at open and close, so any repair a hot pass surfaces spoils the pass that found it -- inherent rather than a fault, and the cheap order is to TERM the spoiled pass at once rather than let it run to a fixed verdict.
obs THE REBASE FOUND A PEER HAD LANDED MY HARNESS REPAIR TWO HOURS AHEAD OF ME, from the same root: a git worktree at .lap/verify doubled the same file for that ship where .lap/norm did for mine. Theirs is upstream and wider, so I kept theirs whole and grafted the one thing it lacked -- the refusal on an exit past 1, which its own probe still hedged.
obs I applied my own paper's lesson to the merge: measure what mine holds that theirs does not. Mine held the refusal, the ignored_read_past count beside their ignored_filtered flag, and the pen's git shim. It did NOT hold a better round-open naming, so I WITHDREW mine -- BAKERY had landed the same reading with the stash named beside the path.
obs One real interaction survived the withdrawal: their naming greps orphan:work anchored to the field end, and my near rows spell orphan:work:near:<path>:<share>, so an anchored match would name every work path except the ones carrying a candidate worth checking. Widened to a prefix.
obs Their eight new control legs stood pinned by nothing -- fail=0 reads the same over a gutted pen -- so I named all eight by ordinal in their witness while I was there.
obs THE FRONT IS FULL: upstream's card read 40,959 of 40,960, so my block had to fit my elder's 1,657-byte footprint rather than the space the finding wanted. Three rewrites went to bytes rather than to meaning, which is the cost PETRICHOR and BAKERY have both named as Keaton's word.
obs I left 20260911 OPEN on both roster pins rather than closing it at 58: nine peer commits arrived in the rebase and the shelf read 67 by the time I looked. A count that moves while I hold it belongs to a later lap.
